      { q: "What is MRP?", a: "**Maximum Retail Price**, the highest price that can be charged for a product; a consumer can bargain for less but cannot be charged more.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "When is World Consumer Rights Day observed?", a: "On **15 March**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Give two reasons why consumers need protection in the marketplace.", a: "1. Individual consumers are **weak** compared with large companies that have huge wealth, power and reach.\n2. Sellers may use **under-weighing, adulteration, false information or defective goods**, so rules and regulations are needed.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State the role of consumer forums or consumer protection councils.", a: "1. They **guide consumers** on how to file cases in consumer courts.\n2. They often **represent consumers** in the courts.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain three consumer rights with one example each.", a: "1. **Right to safety**: protection against hazardous goods, e.g. a pressure cooker with a working safety valve.\n2. **Right to be informed**: about ingredients, price, batch number and expiry date.\n3. **Right to choose**: freedom to decide whether to buy a product, e.g. a gas dealer cannot force a customer to buy a stove.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why is the consumer movement still slow in India? Give three reasons.", a: "1. **Enforcement of laws is weak** and rules for markets are often not followed.\n2. Many consumers are **unaware of their rights**.\n3. Consumer courts are often **cumbersome, expensive and time-consuming**, and shopkeepers often do not issue bills as evidence.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the three-tier redressal machinery under COPRA. Mention its limitations.", a: "1. COPRA set up consumer courts at the **district, state and national** levels.\n2. A case goes to the court depending on the **value of the claim**.\n3. A consumer can approach the court **without a lawyer**.\n4. Limitation: the process is often **cumbersome, expensive and time-consuming**.\n5. Limitation: consumers need bills or cash memos as evidence, and many are unaware of their rights.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Describe the common forms of exploitation of consumers and the ways in which consumers can protect themselves.", a: "1. Forms: **under-weighing**, hidden charges, **adulteration**, **defective goods** and **false information**.\n2. Consumers should check **labels and certification marks** (ISI, Agmark, Hallmark).\n3. They should ask for **bills and cash memos**.\n4. They should know their **rights** (safety, information, choice, redressal, representation).\n5. They should **complain** against exploitation to consumer forums or courts.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Meera bought a bottle of packaged drinking water. The label had no manufacturing date and no ISI mark. When she found it unclean, the shopkeeper refused to give a bill.\n(i) Which mark is mandatory on packaged drinking water?\n(ii) Which consumer right was violated by the absence of the manufacturing date?\n(iii) Where can she file a complaint?", a: "(i) The **ISI mark** (certification by the Bureau of Indian Standards) is mandatory.\n(ii) The **right to be informed**.\n(iii) At the **District Consumer Court**; she should keep whatever proof she has, as a bill is evidence.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
